          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            %em time-ordered product
            is a product on suitably well-behaved
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            which re-orders its arguments according to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+ordering"} causal ordering
            of their spacetime supports before multiplying with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
            product.
          %p
            (The analogue for reverse
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+ordering"} causal ordering
            is called the
            %em reverse-time ordered
            or
            %em anti-time ordered
            product.)
          %p
            For example, for point-evaluation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+observables"} field observables
            and distinct
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/events"} events
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y \in \Sigma
            the time-ordered product is defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Φ
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Φ
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi Φ
                              %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msup
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi Φ
                              %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mtext not in the past of
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mi y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi Φ
                              %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msup
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi Φ
                              %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mtd
                            %mtext otherwise
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T(\mathbf{\Phi}^a(x) \mathbf{\Phi}^b(y))
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\left\{
                  \\array{
                  \\mathbf{\Phi}^a(x) \mathbf{\Phi}^a(y)
                  \&amp;\vert&amp;
                  x \, \text{not in the past of}\, y
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{\Phi}^a(y)
                  \\mathbf{\Phi}^a(x)
                  \&amp;\vert&amp;
                  \\text{otherwise}
                  }
                  \\right.
          %p
            This may be understood as arising from the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+additivity"} causal additivity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+S-matrix"} perturbative S-matrix
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dyson+series"} Dyson series
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %p
            More precisely, the time-ordered product is a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcausal+polynomial+observables"} microcausal polynomial observables
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+state"}> Hadamard state
            ) which on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observables"} regular polynomial observables
            given on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+polynomial+observables"} regular polynomial observables
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            which is induced (via
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "star+product#PropagatorStarProduct"} this def.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            and which is extended from there, in the sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensions+of+distributions"} extensions of distributions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microcausal+polynomial+observables"} microcausal polynomial observables
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %h3#OnRegularPolynomialObservables On regular polynomial observables
          #OnRegularPolynomialObservablesTimeOrderedProduct.num_defn
